import System.IO.Console;
program AgentsExe;

// A colour, and the two things the bake does to one: scale it by a shade factor, and mix it with
// another.
//
// Thirty places wrote the same three lines -- `cast<float>(atlas.cr[i]) / 255.0 * shade` and its
// green and blue twins -- and the triples are where a colour bug hides, because two of the three
// being right is enough to look almost correct. A shade applied to red and green but not blue is a
// tint, and a tint reads as art.
//
// ---- WHY THE COMPONENTS ARE `double`, WHICH IS NOT AN OVERSIGHT ----
//
// `cast<float>(cr) / 255.0 * shade` computes in DOUBLE, because `255.0` is a double literal, and
// truncates to float exactly once when it lands in a float. Storing the components as `float`
// would truncate a second time -- once into the struct and once out of it -- and two roundings are
// not one rounding. The struct holds what the expression holds. This is the same trap the world's
// contour constants sprang, and it cost a red gate to learn the first time; it is written down
// here so it costs nothing the second.
//
// ---- WHY ONE OPERATOR AND NOT THREE ----
//
// Operator overloading is the least-exercised feature in this language and it has produced a
// defect on every implementation it has been given so far, so putting it here was deliberate: an
// operator returning its own struct type, on a value struct living inside a render owner, in the
// one path where every output pixel is compared against a golden.
//
// It carries `*` alone, because that is the only one with a customer. Two were surveyed and
// refused:
//
//   `+`  -- nothing in the bake adds two colours. The only mix in this renderer is `mix()` inside
//           the fragment shader's source string, which runs on the GPU and never touches a struct
//           here. An operator with no caller is an unchecked claim.
//
//   `*=` -- there IS a site shaped exactly like it (`r = r * sh` and its two twins, in the river
//           tile emitter), and it is NOT the same arithmetic. Those locals are `float` and are
//           assigned from other floats, so the value is already rounded to single precision before
//           the multiply; going through this struct would multiply in double and round once, at
//           the end. One rounding is not two. The site keeps its own spelling and this struct does
//           not pretend to cover it.
public bundle Agents {
    public namespace Gfx {

        public struct Rgb {
            public mutable double r;
            public mutable double g;
            public mutable double b;

            public constructor Rgb(double r, double g, double b) {
                this.r = r;
                this.g = g;
                this.b = b;
            }

            // A colour as the atlases store it: three bytes.
            public static method fromBytes(int cr, int cg, int cb) returns Rgb {
                return new Rgb(cast<double>(cr) / 255.0,
                               cast<double>(cg) / 255.0,
                               cast<double>(cb) / 255.0);
            }

            // Scale by a shade factor. Every component or none -- which is the whole reason this
            // is one operation instead of three lines.
            public operator * (double k) returns Rgb {
                return new Rgb(this.r * k, this.g * k, this.b * k);
            }

        }
    }
}
